","Central Denmark Region","DI-ALL","DI-GV","DI-GV-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-OT","DI-OT-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Governmental Institutions","Hospitals","Miscellaneous","Other Hospitals","Other Research","Universities","University of Southern Denmark","danish air ambulance"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-2ada09b6-6dd5-4b3e-a548-5a3e5964e212"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Inter hospital transfers in rotor wing aircraft. Patterns and challenges : Protocol for a scoping review","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-782901","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"Arthritis affects many individuals and can cause pain and limit physical functioning. Exercise is an important treatment option for individuals with arthritis; however, adherence to exercise programs can be challenging. A new initiative in Denmark has introduced nature-based exercises for patients with arthritis. This qualitative study aimed to explore the experiences of the individuals who participated in those exercises. The study used a hermeneutic-phenomenological approach and conducted along-side interviews with 12 women and three men who participated in nature-based exercise programs for arthritis. Analysis of the data revealed two main themes. The first theme highlighted the positive effects of being in nature, such as increased vitality, reduced pain, and a sense of well-being. The second theme emphasized the social benefits of participating in these exercises, including a sense of connection and community with others. Overall, this study suggests that incorporating nature-based exercises in treatment programs for arthritis could have significant benefits for individuals with this condition.","author_count":5,"contributors":["Knudsen, Jannie Buhl","Simonÿ, Charlotte","Tang, Lars Hermann","Skou, Søren Thorgaard","Beck, Malene"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-792106"],"filters":{"added":"2025-02","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"corresponding_author":["Beck, Malene"],"keyword":["Arthritis","Chronic","Lived experience","Natural environment","Nature based exercise","Nature based intervention","Phenomenology","Qualitative","Well being"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-ZEA"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-ZEA","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"nature based group exercises for people with arthritis a qualitative along side interview study of lived experiences","src":["sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85185667941","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-792106","identifier":[{"id":"38360058","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85185667941","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["sdu-ba9dcbc4-5f95-4785-8e4e-7d9f093d6dfb"],"keyword":["along-side interviewing","arthritis","chronic","lived experience","natural environment","nature-based exercise","nature-based intervention","phenomenology","qualitative","well-being"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-E875D34A6626D81CDC43FE61798E3093"],"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"dk","key":"region zealand","l2":"DI-HO","l2n":"Hospitals","l3":"DI-HO-ZEA","l3nl":"Region Zealand","l3nn":"Region Zealand","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Region Zealand","namevar":["Næstved-Slagelse-Ringsted Hospitals"],"nora_code":"DI-HO-ZEA","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"sdu university of southern denmark","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-SDU","l3nl":"University of Southern Denmark","l3nn":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"SDU University of Southern Denmark","namevar":["SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research","SDU, The Faculty of Health Sciences, Department of Regional Health Research, IRS - Næstved, Slagelse, Ringsted Hospitals (NSR), Research Unit of Physiotherapy and Occupational Therapy (Slagelse)"],"nora_code":"DI-U-SDU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Jannie Buhl","key":"knudsenpau","last":"Knudsen","name":"Knudsen, Jannie Buhl","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte","key":"simonÿpau","last":"Simonÿ","locid":"6c2dbf49-1a55-4aa3-a990-1549e39274dd","name":"Simonÿ, Charlotte","orcid":"0000-0003-1189-2967","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Lars Hermann","key":"tangpau","last":"Tang","locid":"c847c05b-1b9e-489f-a928-7f24d665966d","name":"Tang, Lars Hermann","orcid":"0000-0001-6055-8696","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren T.","key":"skoupau","last":"Skou","locid":"656a59cf-32bc-4190-b072-4adf47d954c1","name":"Skou, Søren Thorgaard","name_org":"Skou, Søren T.","orcid":"0000-0003-4336-7059","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 2","corresponding":null,"first":"Malene","key":"beckpau","last":"Beck","locid":"d9d3d0b1-3ebc-42af-bb82-d508d959b3f8","name":"Beck, Malene","orcid":"0000-0003-4163-263X","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["38360058"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1177/10497323241227151","issn":["10497323","15527557"],"issn_org":["10497323"],"issue":"10","journal":"Qualitative Health Research","pages":"951-963","publisher":"SAGE Publications","status":"p","title":"Qualitative Health Research","volume":"34"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85185667941"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Hospitals","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-HO-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Hospitals","Region Zealand","Universities","University of Southern Denmark"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["sdu-ba9dcbc4-5f95-4785-8e4e-7d9f093d6dfb"],"src":["SDU University of Southern Denmark"],"title":"Nature-Based Group Exercises for People With Arthritis : A Qualitative Along-Side Interview Study of Lived Experiences","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-792106","year":"2024","yearsub":"2024"}
{"abstract":"The 2020 onset of the COVID-19 pandemic globally strained healthcare. Healthcare systems worldwide had to rapidly reorganize, impacting service delivery, patient care, and care-seeking behaviors. This left little time to assess the pandemic's effects on patient safety. This paper investigates COVID-19's influence on patient safety in a Danish region, using data from the national reporting system for adverse events during the initial COVID-19 surge in early 2020. This retrospective analysis investigated how the early phase of the COVID-19 pandemic (January-September 2020) affected the incidence of adverse events in a Danish Region, comparing it to the same period in 2019. Data were sourced from the Danish Patient Safety Database and regional systems. Adverse events were reported numerically. Descriptive statistics were employed to describe the percentage difference in adverse events and hospital activity, as well as the rate of adverse events per 1000 activities. Additionally, COVID-19-specific adverse events from April 2020 to March 2021 were identified and analyzed, categorizing them into seven risk areas across various healthcare sectors. During Denmark's initial COVID-19 surge in early 2020, the North Denmark Region's hospitals reported a significant decrease in adverse events, with a 42.5% drop in March 2020 compared to March 2019. From January to September 2020, the number of adverse events dropped 8.5% compared to the same period in 2019. In the same period, hospital activity declined by 10.2%. The ratio of reported adverse events per 1000 hospital activities thus decreased in early 2020 but showed only a minor difference overall for January-September compared to 2019. Between April 2020 and March 2021, out of 5703 total adverse events, 324 (5.7%) were COVID-19 related. COVID-19-related events were categorized into seven distinct risk areas, reflecting diverse impacts across healthcare sectors including hospitals, general practices, pre-hospital care, and specialized services. The initial decline in reporting of adverse events likely resulted from rapid healthcare changes and under-prioritization of the reporting system during the acute phase. However, a near return to pre-pandemic reporting levels suggests a resilient reporting system despite the crisis. The study's strength lies in the comprehensive data from Danish reporting systems, though it acknowledges potential underreporting and doesn't measure the pandemic's overall impact on patient safety.","author_count":9,"contributors":["Uggerby, Christian","Knudsen, Søren Valgreen","Grøntved, Simon","Sørensen, Agnete Lund","Lyng Larsen, Karen","Schmidt, Charlotte Kaae","Jensen, Tilde","Pedersen, Jens Ravnholt","Mainz, Jan"],"cuid":["S02-100.00-767000"],"filters":{"added":"2024-09","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","class":"merged","cluster_size":2,"continent":["Asia, Middle East","OECD"],"corresponding_author":["Knudsen, Søren Valgreen"],"country":["Israel"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Haifa"],"keyword":["Adverse events","COVID 19 epidemiology","Covid 19","Delivery of Health Care","Denmark epidemiology","Healthcare system","Humans","Pandemic","Pandemics","Patient safety","Retrospective Studies","SARS CoV 2"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-HO","DI-U"],"level3":{"HO":["DI-HO-ALL","DI-HO-NDR"],"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL","DI-U-SDU"]},"level3all":["DI-HO-NDR","DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"adverse events reporting during the covid 000000000019 pandemic in a danish region a retrospective analysis","src":["aau","sdu"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-SDU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"http://www.scopus.com/inward/record.url?scp=85196137925&partnerID=8YFLogxK","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S02-100.00-767000","identifier":[{"id":"85196137925","type":"scopus"},{"id