{"abstract":"A water purification system is typically used in PEM water electrolysis systems, but over time the quality of the circulating water may deteriorate due to metallic impurities originating from the piping system, catalyst layers and other components degradation. These impurities lead to a progressive increase of the cell voltage [1]. In this work, the influence of 5 ppm Fe3+ contamination on cell performance of PEM water electrolysis has been investigated, and the temperature and current density effects on cell performance in the presence of Fe3+ were also studied.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Li, Na","Araya, Samuel Simon","Kær, Søren Knudsen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-056777"],"event":{"end":"2019-06-13","place":"Loen, no","start":"2019-06-09","title":"2nd International Conference on Electrolysis 2019","year":"2019"},"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Ferric ion impurity","Water purification system"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"the influence of ferric ion impurities on a proton exchange membrane electrolyzer operated at varying temperature and current density conditions","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dco","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/308805564/ICE_Poster_Na.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-056777","ids":["aau-16e300c2-873a-450a-aa5d-4bf4edf43274"],"keyword":["Ferric ion impurity","Water purification system"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-F85C8181AB1BAE8413BF83498FDDE973"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/308805564/ICE_Poster_Na.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy, Hydrogen and Electro-fuels","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy, Thermal Energy Systems","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy, Thermofluids"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Na","key":"lipau","last":"Li","locid":"a89f75fa-7daa-406c-b5ea-5307f875cdf0","name":"Li, Na","orcid":"0000-0002-1267-6185","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Samuel","key":"simonarayapau","last":"Simon Araya","locid":"31cc4ce7-c9ae-4406-95d1-b95d4269205f","name":"Araya, Samuel Simon","name_org":"Simon Araya, Samuel","orcid":"0000-0001-9294-0793","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Søren Knudsen","key":"kærpau","last":"Kær","locid":"898d6584-ec2e-430a-b78d-e3e0d670dcdd","name":"Kær, Søren Knudsen","orcid":"0000-0003-2151-8055","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-16e300c2-873a-450a-aa5d-4bf4edf43274"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"The influence of ferric ion impurities on a proton exchange membrane electrolyzer operated at varying temperature and current density conditions","type":"Conference Poster","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-056777","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"In this paper, the main faults in a commercial proton exchange membrane fuel cell (PEMFC) stack for micro-combined heat and power (m-CHP) application are investigated, with the scope of experimentally identifying fault indicators for diagnosis purposes. The tested faults were reactant starvation (both fuel and oxidant), flooding, drying, CO poisoning, and H 2 S poisoning. Galvanostatic electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were recorded between 2 kHz and 0.1 Hz on a commercial stack of 46 cells of a 100-cm2 active area each. The results, obtained through distribution of relaxation time (DRT) analysis of the EIS data, show that characteristic peaks of the DRT and their changes with the different fault intensity levels can be used to extract the features of the tested faults. It was shown that flooding and drying present features on the opposite ends of the frequency spectrum due the effect of drying on the membrane conductivity and the blocking effect of flooding that constricts the reactants' flow. Moreover, it was seen that while the effect of CO poisoning is limited to high frequency processes, above 100 Hz, the effects of H 2 S extend to below 10 Hz. Finally, the performance degradation due to all the tested faults, including H 2 S poisoning, is recoverable to a great extent, implying that condition correction after fault detection can contribute to prolonged lifetime of the fuel cell.","author_count":6,"contributors":["Araya, Samuel Simon","Zhou, Fan","Sahlin, Simon Lennart","Thomas, Sobi","Jeppesen, Christian","Kær, Søren Knudsen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-090975"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"6-10 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Diagnosis","Distribution of relaxation times","Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy","Fault","Fuel cells"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"fault characterization of a proton exchange membrane fuel cell stack","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/en12010152","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-090975","identifier":[{"id":"000460665000152","type":"wos"},{"id":"85060006601","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-8cd47605-0d42-42c5-abd0-0fe0aa5debbb"],"keyword":["Diagnosis","Distribution of relaxation times","Electrochemical impedance spectroscopy","Fault","Fuel cells"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-7A05F5FB3C0688593C14D466A8D55F20"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.3390/en12010152","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/321417884/energies_12_00152_v2.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy, Hydrogen and Electro-fuels","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy, Thermal Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Samuel","key":"simonarayapau","last":"Simon Araya","locid":"31cc4ce7-c9ae-4406-95d1-b95d4269205f","name":"Araya, Samuel Simon","name_org":"Simon Araya, Samuel","orcid":"0000-0001-9294-0793","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Fan","key":"zhoupau","last":"Zhou","locid":"874642c9-fe83-4b87-9b5e-9b8088c39e6a","name":"Zhou, Fan","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Simon Lennart","key":"sahlinpau","last":"Sahlin","locid":"10be1b68-a59d-4397-ae45-e356bf4c5844","name":"Sahlin, Simon Lennart","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Sobi","key":"thomaspau","last":"Thomas","locid":"33bb2f77-e034-426b-ac72-d921d4354655","name":"Thomas, Sobi","orcid":"0000-0002-2759-3753","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Christian","key":"jeppesenpau","last":"Jeppesen","locid":"0e062d6c-eeae-49d3-ae56-d750f51b90b7","name":"Jeppesen, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-0297-6854","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren Knudsen","key":"kærpau","last":"Kær","locid":"898d6584-ec2e-430a-b78d-e3e0d670dcdd","name":"Kær, Søren Knudsen","orcid":"0000-0003-2151-8055","rank":6,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.3390/en12010152","issn":["19961073"],"issue":"1","journal":"Energies","pages":"1-17","paperid":"en12010152","publisher":"MDPI AG","publisher_org":"M D P I AG","status":"p","title":"Energies","volume":"12"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85060006601"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-8cd47605-0d42-42c5-abd0-0fe0aa5debbb"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Fault Characterization of a Proton Exchange Membrane Fuel Cell Stack","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-090975","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"In this work, a state-of-the-art, full-scale, Proton Exchange Membrane (PEM) electrolysis cell model is presented. The developed three-dimensional (3D) model accounts for compressible, two-phase flow including species, heat and charge transport in the anode and membrane. By incorporating electrochemistry as well as detailed heat and two-phase flow transport phenomena, the model is capable of studying cells at full-scale and for high current densities with high accuracy. To enable the modeling of thin catalyst layers (CL) for high current density operation in a 3D framework, the CL is modeled as an interfacial boundary. The necessary electrochemical parameters are obtained by fitting polarization curves of a two-dimensional version of the devised model to experimental measurements from a small cell. It is found that the obtained parameters are in agreement with literature values and that the fitted model is able to capture the performance for temperatures from 323 to 353 K and for current densities up to 5 A cm −2. Furthermore, it is identified that for high current density operation, three types of overpotential losses are nearly equally dominant: the anode kinetics, the PEM ohmic resistance and the non-membrane ohmic resistance due to poor electrical contact between layers and current constrictions in the CL. The developed 3D model is applied to three different circular, interdigitated anode flow fields aimed a high pressure and high current density operation. When operating the cell at a cathode pressure of 100 bar, a current density of 5 A cm −2 and stoichiometric constant of 350, it is found that the cell potential shows little dependence on the applied flow field. However, large in-plane variations can occur that may impact lifetime significantly. Particularly for the temperature field, an in-plane difference of up to 20.2 K relative to the intended cell temperature is found in the worst case. For all three cases, the occurrence of hot spots is linked to the maldistribution of two-phase flow and current density. Out of the studied cases, it was found that equal land width between the channels gives the best distribution of charge, mass and heat.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Olesen, Anders Christian","Frensch, Steffen Henrik","Kær, Søren Knudsen"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-111968"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Electrolysis","Full scale","Gas liquid flow","High current density","Modeling","PEM"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-AAU","DI-U-ALL"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AAU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"towards uniformly distributed heat mass and charge a flow field design study for high pressure and high current density operation of pem electrolysis cells","src":["aau"],"src-code":["DI-U-AAU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/288339784/manuscript_EA_EA18_3894.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-111968","identifier":[{"id":"85054835249","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["aau-d538f948-188e-40b7-931f-d6e30d7f8c5d"],"keyword":["Electrolysis","Full-scale","Gas-liquid flow","High current density","Modeling","PEM"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-0796DE938007BA24D059C8EF8DC5F9C7"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY-NC-ND 4.0","type":"loc","url":"https://vbn.aau.dk/ws/files/288339784/manuscript_EA_EA18_3894.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"aau aalborg university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AAU","l3nl":"Aalborg University","l3nn":"AAU Aalborg University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AAU Aalborg University","namevar":["Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy","Aalborg University, The Faculty of Engineering and Science, AAU Energy, Thermal Engineering"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AAU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Anders Christian","key":"olesenpau","last":"Olesen","locid":"a6c83a7f-2f25-4b66-b42d-9ef435ffeecf","name":"Olesen, Anders Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-8344-914X","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Steffen Henrik","key":"frenschpau","last":"Frensch","locid":"81f53416-7408-4e66-9964-11ce7c6aafc9","name":"Frensch, Steffen Henrik","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Søren Knudsen","key":"kærpau","last":"Kær","locid":"898d6584-ec2e-430a-b78d-e3e0d670dcdd","name":"Kær, Søren Knudsen","orcid":"0000-0003-2151-8055","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1016/j.electacta.2018.10.008","issn":["00134686","18733859"],"issn_org":["00134686"],"journal":"Electrochimica Acta","pages":"476-495","publisher":"Elsevier","status":"p","title":"Electrochimica Acta","volume":"293"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85054835249"],"search":{"DAF":["Aalborg University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["aau-d538f948-188e-40b7-931f-d6e30d7f8c5d"],"src":["AAU Aalborg University"],"title":"Towards uniformly distributed heat, mass and charge : A flow field design study for high pressure and high current density operation of PEM electrolysis cells","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-111968","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"Introduction: Diabetic kidney disease (DKD) is a major cause of morbidity and mortality in diabetes and is the most common cause of proteinuric and non-proteinuric forms of end-stage renal disease (ESRD). Control of risk factors such as blood glucose and blood pressure is not always achievable or effective. Significant research efforts have attempted to understand the pathophysiology of DKD and develop new therapies. Areas covered: We review DKD pathophysiology in the context of existing and emerging therapies that affect hemodynamic and metabolic pathways. Renin-angiotensin system (RAS) inhibition has become standard care. Recent evidence for renoprotective activity of SGLT2 inhibitors and GLP-1 agonists is an exciting step forward while endothelin receptor blockade shows promise. Multiple metabolic pathways of DKD have been evaluated with varying success; including mitochondrial function, reactive oxygen species, NADPH oxidase (NOX), transcription factors (NF-B and Nrf2), advanced glycation, protein kinase C (PKC), aldose reductase, JAK-STAT, autophagy, apoptosis-signaling kinase 1 (ASK1), fibrosis and epigenetics. Expert opinion: There have been major advances in the understanding and treatment of DKD. SGLT2i and GLP-1 agonists have demonstrated renoprotection, with novel therapies under evaluation. Addressing the interaction between hemodynamic and metabolic pathways may help achieve prevention, attenuation or even reversal of DKD.","author_count":3,"contributors":["Warren, Annabelle M.","Knudsen, Søren Tang","Cooper, Mark E."],"cuid":["S01-000.00-172439"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["OECD","Oceania"],"corresponding_author":["Cooper, Mark E."],"country":["Australia"],"glorg":["Alfred Hospital","All Global Affiliations","Monash University"],"keyword":["Diabetic complications","Diabetic kidney disease","Diabetic nephropathy","Prevention","Therapeutic targets","renoprotection"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"diabetic nephropathy an insight into molecular mechanisms and emerging therapies","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"un","type":"url","url":"https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85066611717","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-172439","identifier":[{"id":"85066611717","type":"scopus"},{"id":"31154867","type":"pmid"}],"ids":["au-4ba9d851-36f9-4a9d-8a4b-0072400c17d0"],"keyword":["Diabetic kidney disease","diabetic complications","diabetic nephropathy","prevention","renoprotection","therapeutic targets"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Medical Science"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-B91413EDE0F76C3E7DE1693ABE568AF7"],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Medicine (MED)"],"yearPub":"2019","yearSub":"2019"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"au","key":"alfred hospital","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Alfred Hospital","used":1},{"affno":"2","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Health, Department of Clinical Medicine, Department of Clinical Medicine - SDCA-Steno Diabetes Center Aarhus"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1},{"affno":"3","country":"au","key":"monash university","loc":["Australia","Oceania","OECD"],"name":"Monash University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Annabelle M.","key":"warrenpau","last":"Warren","name":"Warren, Annabelle M.","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2 3","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Søren T.","key":"knudsenpau","last":"Knudsen","locid":"f4fb113c-f8d2-4bb3-9a9d-0bc2ef14590d","name":"Knudsen, Søren Tang","name_org":"Knudsen, Søren T.","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1 3","corresponding":null,"first":"Mark E.","key":"cooperpau","last":"Cooper","name":"Cooper, Mark E.","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pmid":["31154867"],"pub":{"doi":"10.1080/14728222.2019.1624721","issn":["14728222","17447631"],"issn_org":["14728222"],"issue":"7","journal":"Expert Opinion On Therapeutic Targets","pages":"579-591","publisher":"Taylor and Francis Ltd.","status":"p","title":"Expert Opinion On Therapeutic Targets","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85066611717"],"search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-4ba9d851-36f9-4a9d-8a4b-0072400c17d0"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Diabetic nephropathy : an insight into molecular mechanisms and emerging therapies","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-172439","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"author_count":5,"contributors":["Knudsen, Hanne","Christensen, Søren"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-208532"],"filters":{"added":"2021-10","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Læring","Motivation"],"level":"sci","mra":["hum"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"komme først eller være god konkurrence som prekær motivation for læring","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dba","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-208532","ids":["au-8ad0f779-70c3-4883-aed0-2742fc1ab744"],"keyword":["Læring","Motivation"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Humanities"],"noi":["Local-1-1-646237FB8F1D25D2ED829B2F2EAB36AB"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"au aarhus university","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-AU","l3nl":"Aarhus University","l3nn":"AU Aarhus University","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"AU Aarhus University","namevar":["Aarhus University, Arts, Danish School of Education, Danish School of Education - Education Studies, Emdrup"],"nora_code":"DI-U-AU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Hanne","key":"knudsenpau","last":"Knudsen","locid":"64613f11-d8e3-46a7-9eae-4a4a50cf9fdc","name":"Knudsen, Hanne","orcid":"0000-0003-3879-0629","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"country":"dk","first":"Søren","key":"christensenpau","last":"Christensen","locid":"c6bf7b94-cdf4-4abe-8e80-5526aacfd66c","name":"Christensen, Søren","orcid":"0000-0002-1335-0806","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"","first":"Kristine","key":"kousholtpedh","last":"Kousholt","name":"Kousholt, Kristine","rank":3,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"John Benedicto","key":"krejslerpedh","last":"Krejsler","name":"Krejsler, John Benedicto","rank":4,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"},{"affno":"","first":"Morten","key":"nissenpedh","last":"Nissen","name":"Nissen, Morten","rank":5,"role":"Editor of Host Publication","rolecode":"pedh"}],"pub":{"eisbn":["9788759335543"],"isbn":["9788759332870"],"pages":"149-172","place":"Frederiksberg","publisher":"Samfundslitteratur","status":"p","subtitle":"om motivationens tilblivelse","title":"At skulle ville"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["Aarhus University","All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["au-8ad0f779-70c3-4883-aed0-2742fc1ab744"],"src":["AU Aarhus University"],"title":"Komme først eller være god? : konkurrence som prekær motivation for læring","type":"Book Chapter","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-208532","year":"2019","yearsub":"2019"}
{"abstract":"PURPOSE: Novel sensitive methods for early detection of relapse and for monitoring therapeutic efficacy may have a huge impact on risk stratification, treatment, and ultimately outcome for patients with bladder cancer. We addressed the prognostic and predictive impact of ultra-deep sequencing of cell-free DNA in patients before and after cystectomy and during chemotherapy. PATIENTS AND METHODS: We included 68 patients with localized advanced bladder cancer. Patient-specific somatic mutations, identified by whole-exome sequencing, were used to assess circulating tumor DNA (ctDNA) by ultra-deep sequencing (median, 105,000×) of plasma DNA. Plasma samples (n = 656) were procured at diagnosis, during chemotherapy, before cystectomy, and during surveillance. Expression profiling was performed for tumor subtype and immune signature analyses. RESULTS: Presence of ctDNA was highly prognostic at diagnosis before chemotherapy (hazard ratio, 29.1; P = .001). After cystectomy, ctDNA analysis correctly identified all patients with metastatic relapse during disease monitoring (100% sensitivity, 98% specificity). A median lead time over radiographic imaging of 96 days was observed. In addition, for high-risk patients (ctDNA positive before or during treatment), the dynamics of ctDNA during chemotherapy was associated with disease recurrence (P = .023), whereas pathologic downstaging was not. Analysis of tumor-centric biomarkers showed that mutational processes (signature 5) were associated with pathologic downstaging (P = .024); however, no significant correlation for tumor subtypes, DNA damage response mutations, and other biomarkers was observed. Our results suggest that ctDNA analysis is better associated with treatment efficacy compared with other available methods. CONCLUSION: ctDNA assessment for early risk stratification, therapy monitoring, and early relapse detection in bladder cancer is feasible and provides a basis for clinical studies that evaluate early therapeutic interventions.","author_count":34,"contributors":["Christensen, Emil","Birkenkamp-Demtröder, Karin","Sethi, Himanshu","Shchegrova, Svetlana","Salari, Raheleh","Nordentoft, Iver","Wu, Hsin-Ta","Knudsen, Michael","Lamy, Philippe","Lindskrog, Sia Viborg","Taber, Ann","Balcioglu, Mustafa","Vang, Søren","Assaf, Zoe","Sharma, Shruti","Tin, Antony S","Srinivasan, Ramya","Hafez, Dina","Reinert, Thomas","Navarro, Samantha","Olson, Alexander","Ram, Rosalyn","Dashner, Scott","Rabinowitz, Matthew","Billings, Paul","Sigurjonsson, Styrmir","Andersen, Claus Lindbjerg","Swenerton, Ryan","Aleshin, Alexey","Zimmermann, Bernhard","Agerbæk, Mads","Lin, Cheng-Ho Jimmy","Jensen, Jørgen Bjerggaard","Dyrskjøt, Lars"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-228869"],"filters":{"added":"2021-12","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"11-50 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cancer","Cisplatin","Clonal Evolution","Liquid biopsies","Neoadjuvant chemotherapy","Recurrence","SOMATIC ERCC2 MUTATIONS","Treatment response","circulating tumor DNA"],"level":"sci","mra":["med"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-OT","DI-U"],"level3":{"OT":["Unspecified organisation"],"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-AU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-AU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"early detection of metastatic relapse and monitoring of therapeutic efficacy by ultra deep sequencing of plasma cell free dna in patients with urothelial bladder carcinoma","src":["au"],"src-code":["DI-U-AU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.18.02052","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-228869","identifier":[{"id":"000474713000005","type":"wos"},{"id":"31059311","type":"pmid"},{"id":"85068427517","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["au-ae7edf8e-e90c-4709-9f9c-20a1316cbc31"],"keyword":["CANCER","CIRCULATING TUMOR DNA","CISPLATIN","CLONAL EVOLUTION","LIQUID BIOPSIES","NEOADJUVANT CHEMOTHERAPY","RECURRENCE","SOMATI